 [[\textbf{First, the questions we care about may never resolve.}]] Scoring rules and prediction markets require [[\emph{verifiable or falsifiable}]] (VF) events: questions must eventually resolve so that correct answers can be rewarded and bets can be settled. Other sorts of questions, like about counterfactuals, latent variables, human values, [[``]]subjective[['']] questions and so on aren’t VF, in the sense that there isn’t a bet you can settle. [[\citet{conitzerPredictionMarketsMechanism2012}]] formalized prediction markets from the perspective of mechanism design and cooperative game theory, and suggested a [[\emph{two-layer}]] approach to reward agents for [[\emph{information}]] that cannot be directly attributed to their actions: in the [[\emph{bottom}]] layer, a prediction market prices claims about VF events; in the top layer, rewards are distributed to informants based on the cooperative game measuring each agent’s marginal impact on the predictions in the bottom layer. This ensures that each agent gets paid for information that cannot be directly attributed to their actions, as long as the [[\emph{bottom}]] layer ultimately relies on VF events. [[\cref{part:oversight}]] asks what can be done if even this is not available.
